 I. Hosting Plan for Application Components

The application components will be hosted using a cloud-based and containerized deployment model to ensure scalability, reliability, and fault tolerance.

 Proposed Hosting Environment

Cloud Provider: Amazon Web Services

Compute Server: Amazon EC2 Virtual Machine

Containerization: Docker

Code Repository: GitHub

CI/CD Automation: GitHub Actions or Jenkins

Log Storage: Cloud storage or server-based log directory

Deployment Target: Docker containers running on EC2

 Component-wise Hosting Plan
Component	Hosting Location
CI/CD Pipeline Controller	EC2 Server
Build & Test Module	Docker Container on EC2
Log Analyzer	Docker Container on EC2
Failure Classifier	Same EC2 Instance
Recovery Manager	Same EC2 Instance
Notification Module	Cloud-based Email API
Code Repository	GitHub
Deployment Environment	Docker Containers

This architecture ensures:

Easy scaling

Isolation

High availability

 II. Deployment Strategy

The deployment strategy includes automated configuration, containerization, and secure communication.

Step 1: Server Configuration

Launch an EC2 instance in AWS.

Install required tools:

Docker

Python runtime

Git

Configure firewall (Security Groups):

Allow only HTTP (80) and HTTPS (443).

Restrict SSH access to authorized IPs.

This ensures a secure and controlled environment.

 Step 2: Containerization

Create Dockerfiles for all application modules.

Package the following components:

CI/CD controller

Log Analyzer

Recovery Manager

Build Docker images.

Push images to Docker registry (e.g., Docker Hub).

Run containers on the EC2 server.

This ensures:

Consistent environment

Faster deployment

Easy scalability

 Step 3: CI/CD Setup

Connect the GitHub repository to the CI/CD tool.

Configure automated pipeline:

Build → Test → Deploy → Log Analysis → Recovery.

The pipeline automatically triggers when new code is pushed.

This improves:

Automation

Continuous delivery

Reliability

 Step 4: API Configuration and Communication (Important)

To enable communication between different system components, RESTful APIs will be configured.

 API Communication Plan:

Each module exposes REST APIs using HTTP/HTTPS.

JSON is used as the data exchange format.

Internal communication occurs between backend components.

External communication includes frontend and notification services.

 Example Communication Flow:

The frontend sends a request to the backend API to start the pipeline:

POST /start-pipeline

The backend runs the build and test stages.

If a failure occurs, the backend sends logs to the log analyzer:

POST /analyze

The log analyzer processes the logs and returns the failure type.

The recovery module takes appropriate action.

The notification module sends alerts using an email API.

 API Configuration Details:

Define API endpoints and routes for each component.

Configure URLs and ports for services.

Use environment variables to store service URLs.

Enable Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS) for frontend-backend interaction.

Secure APIs using authentication tokens or API keys.

Use HTTPS for encrypted communication.

This ensures:

Seamless integration

Modular architecture

Microservices-based communication

Scalability and flexibility

 Step 5: Monitoring and Logging

Logs are stored in the server directory or cloud storage.

The recovery system continuously monitors logs.

Alerts are generated for failures.

This improves:

Observability

System reliability

 III. Security Measures

To ensure system security and data protection, the following measures will be applied:

Use HTTPS encryption for all API communication.

Configure firewalls and AWS security groups.

Allow only required ports.

Use secure SSH with key-based authentication.

Store sensitive credentials in environment variables.

Implement role-based access control in the CI/CD tool.

Apply authentication and authorization for APIs.

Enable logging and monitoring for suspicious activities.

These measures ensure:

Confidentiality

Integrity

Availability
